## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about combined implications and negation, and to design a small exhaustive search with pruning.

## Key Concepts

- Implication and negation in boolean constraints
- Exhaustive search with pruning
- Small-scope backtracking for correctness checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve() {
  for (let A = 0; A <= 1; A++) {
    for (let B = 0; B <= 1; B++) {
      for (let C = 0; C <= 1; C++) {
        const ok1 = (!A) || B;     // A -> B
        const ok2 = (!B) || (!C);  // B -> ¬C
        const ok3 = (A || B || C); // at least one true
        if (ok1 && ok2 && ok3) return {A,B,C};
      }
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new, well-defined operator (Maj) within Kleene-like three-valued logic and requires concrete CSP reasoning with backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- a custom 3-ary Maj operator (majority with T/F bias)
- constraint propagation and backtracking in non-binary domains

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function maj(a,b,c){
  const t=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='T').length;
  const f=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='F').length;
  if(t>=2) return 'T';
  if(f>=2) return 'F';
  return 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend Maj to 4 inputs while preserving monotonicity?
- What is the impact of introducing additional unknowns on backtracking complexity?
